# Release manifest. One file per release; never edit a published one, add a new .<n> instead.
SWIFT_VERSION=6.4.1
RELEASE_ID=6.4.1-hyp3r.1
BASE_IMAGE=swift:6.4.1-noble
BASE_IMAGE_SLIM=swift:6.4.1-noble-slim
LIBDISPATCH_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-corelibs-libdispatch
LIBDISPATCH_REF=fefb48d71a074c051a05f2e91dff8cf8a4b4e3ad
FOUNDATION_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-corelibs-foundation
FOUNDATION_REF=2c9560ff741797a610e75e88c172d458f79da26d
SWIFT_FOUNDATION_TAG=swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
SWIFT_FOUNDATION_ICU_TAG=swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
SWIFT_COLLECTIONS_TAG=1.1.6
FIXES="Swift 6.4.1 with the hyp3r fixes. SC-0001: libdispatch use-after-free in the Linux epoll backend, SIGSEGV in _dispatch_event_loop_drain (swiftlang/swift-corelibs-libdispatch#949); backport of upstream PR #965 onto swift-6.4.1-RELEASE. SC-0002: Foundation Process on Linux over-reads dirent.d_name in findMaximumOpenFromProcSelfFD, intermittent SIGSEGV (swiftlang/swift-corelibs-foundation#5472); backport of upstream commit 81eb85a2 onto swift-6.4.1-RELEASE. SC-0004: the optimizer moved a class field-offset load above the allocation of the first instance (metadata accessors are modelled as readnone), so the first object of a class with a non-constant layout could get a field store over its isa pointer, intermittent SIGSEGV or silent heap corruption in -O builds; fixed in swift-frontend (LLVM alias analysis of swift_allocObject), the Linux images and tarballs carry the rebuilt compiler SC-0006: #isolation in nonisolated(nonsending) code (the default under NonisolatedNonsendingByDefault) ignored its contextual type, e.g. #isolation === MainActor.shared, so -O builds aborted in the LLVM verifier ('PHINode should have one entry for each predecessor', swiftlang/swift#92798); now converted, and types (any Actor)? cannot convert to are diagnosed like in @MainActor code (#isolation != nil there becomes an error; write (#isolation as (any Actor)?) != nil). SC-0019: #isolation in nonisolated(nonsending) code was consumed without a copy, so e.g. String(describing: #isolation) over-released the calling actor (deinit while running, then SIGSEGV; swiftlang/swift#92799). Both fixed in swift-frontend; the Linux images and tarballs carry the rebuilt compiler. SC-0005: a typed-throws closure passed to a generic untyped-throws parameter (e.g. Task { () async throws(E) -> T in ... }) crashed the compiler for error types with storage and returned a corrupted error for small enums (swiftlang/swift#92312, a 6.4 regression); fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0003: -O builds of async functions that take a small enum or struct whose native explosion is coerced through a stack temporary (e.g. an enum with a tuple payload) could lose their spilled self and arguments: IRGen sized the temporary's lifetime markers in bits instead of bytes, so after the temporary moved into the async frame the optimizer deleted the spills, and the first resume read garbage (SIGSEGV or wrong values); backport of upstream swiftlang/swift#91312 (main only), fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0022: a typed-throws closure whose context type is a type parameter (stored in [() throws(E) -> T], passed through a generic id<T>) crashed the compiler in SILGen; fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0014: on Linux a DispatchSource signal source never fired once the signal had been set to SIG_IGN (or SIG_DFL) again after the first source for it registered (libdispatch installed its sigaction only once per signal, so the kernel discarded the signal); libdispatch now reinstalls its handler on every registration when the disposition is SIG_IGN/SIG_DFL (fix in src/event/event_epoll.c, not upstream yet); also in the static Linux SDK's libdispatch.a. SC-0023: our SC-0006 fix made every call that passes #isolation to an isolated parameter in nonisolated(nonsending) code count as an isolation crossing, so valid code that stock 6.4.1 accepts was rejected ('non-Sendable ... returned from actor-isolated ... to nonisolated context', e.g. for await over non-Sendable elements, Swift Testing's #expect(processExitsWith:), or 'cannot be called from outside of the actor' for synchronous callees); a regression of hyp3r compilers since Linux hyp3r.9 / macOS hyp3r.8, fixed in swift-frontend. SC-0024: with the SC-0001 libdispatch, SwiftPM's plugin runner kept reading a plugin's stdout/stderr pipes at end-of-file in a tight loop (one CPU busy after every plugin run, e.g. +68% CPU per re-plan); usr/bin/swift-package is rebuilt from the patched SwiftPM fork (swift-build, swift-test, swift-run are symlinks to it). sourcekit-lsp links SwiftPM statically and is not rebuilt."
# Patched Static Linux SDK (musl): the official bundle below with libdispatch.a and libFoundation.a rebuilt from the refs above.
STATIC_SDK_VERSION=0.1.0
STATIC_SDK_URL=https://download.swift.org/swift-6.4.1-release/static-sdk/swift-6.4.1-RELEASE/swift-6.4.1-RELEASE_static-linux-0.1.0.artifactbundle.tar.gz
STATIC_SDK_SHA256=cef5592d7430ed3c5ae1ddda479849c6f5252bd94a5723db1559f984caf38a7c
# Compiler overlay (SC-0003, SC-0004, SC-0005, SC-0006, SC-0019, SC-0022, SC-0023): swift-frontend rebuilt from the ref below plus the unmodified swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
# LLVM/Clang/cmark/swift-syntax/string-processing trees, release build without assertions, swapped into the official toolchain.
# The static SDK's own libraries do not depend on the compiler fix; code built against it still uses the host compiler.
SWIFT_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift
SWIFT_REF=b09c7be428f1a5d4bca0e9a3a3b500e91ecc44a3
SWIFT_UPSTREAM_TAG=swift-6.4.1-RELEASE
# SwiftPM overlay (SC-0024): usr/bin/swift-package rebuilt from the ref below (swift-6.4.1-RELEASE + the fix), recipe in overlays/swiftpm.
SWIFTPM_REPO=Hyp3rSon1X/swift-package-manager
SWIFTPM_REF=4429e8becef360e2a26f4797caa8eb245198f07a
